<%= render "shared/page_header",
      title: "Anatomy & Biology",
      intro: "From the trunk to the tusks to the toes, elephant bodies are full of unusual adaptations." %>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Size: height and weight</h2>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Species</th>
        <th>Shoulder height (adult male)</th>
        <th>Weight (adult male)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>African savanna elephant</strong></td>
        <td>3–4 m (10–13 ft)</td>
        <td>4,500–6,800 kg</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>African forest elephant</strong></td>
        <td>2.2–2.5 m (7–8 ft)</td>
        <td>2,000–4,000 kg</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Asian elephant</strong></td>
        <td>2.5–3 m (8–10 ft)</td>
        <td>3,500–5,000 kg</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Females are noticeably smaller than males. A newborn calf weighs about 100 kg (220 lb) and stands roughly 1 m tall.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Life and breathing</h2>
  <ul class="fact-list">
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Lifespan</span>
      <span class="fact-value">
        In the wild, elephants typically live <strong>60–70 years</strong>. Their lifespan is limited partly by their teeth:
        an elephant goes through six sets of molars, and once the last set wears out it can no longer chew properly.
      </span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Pregnancy</span>
      <span class="fact-value">About <strong>22 months</strong>, the longest of any mammal.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Breathing</span>
      <span class="fact-value">
        Elephants breathe mainly through their trunk, which is a long nose. Unlike most mammals, their lungs are attached directly to the chest wall
        rather than surrounded by a fluid-filled space. This lets them breathe while underwater: swimming elephants hold the tip of their
        trunk above the surface like a snorkel. They can also breathe through their mouth.
      </span>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Unique physical characteristics</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>The trunk</h3>
      <p>
        A fusion of the nose and upper lip with around 40,000 muscle bundles and no bones. Elephants use it to breathe, smell, drink,
        grab food, greet each other and spray dust or water. African elephants have two finger-like tips on the end, Asian elephants one.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Tusks</h3>
      <p>
        Tusks are enormous incisor teeth that keep growing throughout life. They're used for digging, stripping bark, lifting and defence.
        Like people, elephants can be left- or right-tusked.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Ears</h3>
      <p>Large ears full of blood vessels work like radiators. Flapping them cools the blood, which helps an elephant shed heat.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Feet</h3>
      <p>Elephants walk on tiptoe, supported by a thick fatty pad that cushions each step. Their feet can pick up rumbles that travel through the ground.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Skin</h3>
      <p>Up to 2.5 cm thick in places, yet sensitive to touch and sun. Deep wrinkles hold water and mud to keep the skin cool and protected.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Brain</h3>
      <p>At about 5 kg, the elephant brain is the largest of any land animal, and elephants are known for their excellent long-term memory.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure class="photo-block">
    <%= render "shared/image_placeholder", label: "Anatomy diagram coming soon" %>
    <figcaption>The main parts of an elephant's body.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>
